The description confirms the current structure and the field name `Readiness`. I need to patch this field.

Since the patch failed previously, I will use `kubectl set-readiness-probe` if available, but standard kubectl doesn't have this. I must stick to `kubectl patch`.

I will try to patch the specific container's readiness probe using a full replacement in the JSON patch, ensuring I specify the container name "app".

```json
{
  "spec": {
    "template": {
      "spec": {
        "containers": [
          {
            "name": "app",
            "readinessProbe": {
              "httpGet": {
                "path": "/",
                "port": 80
              },
              "initialDelaySeconds": 0,
              "timeoutSeconds": 1,
              "periodSeconds": 10,
              "successThreshold": 1,
              "failureThreshold": 3
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  }
}
```

I will use the `-o yaml` output from `describe` as a basis for a more robust patch, using `kubectl patch --type=merge` again, but making the JSON strictly compliant. I must ensure that I include all the existing probe parameters I want to keep (initialDelaySeconds, timeoutSeconds, etc.) along with the changed path, to avoid accidentally wiping other configuration.

Since the previous merge patch failed, I will assume the failure was due to how I tried to target the list element. I'll use the full patch structure and rely on `merge`.

```bash
kubectl patch deployment app --type='merge' -p '{"spec":{"template":{"spec":{"containers":[{"name":"app","readinessProbe":{"httpGet":{"path":"/"}} ]}}}}'
```
Wait, I must include the existing probe values if I only change the path, otherwise `kubectl` might try to overwrite the whole probe definition with just the path, potentially losing other parameters like `periodSeconds`.

Let's take the existing probe from the description:
`Readiness: http-get http://:80/readyz delay=0s timeout=1s period=10s successThreshold=1 failureThreshold=3`

And modify it to:
`Readiness: http-get http://:80/ delay=0s timeout=1s period=10s successThreshold=1 failureThreshold=3`

I will replace the whole readiness probe object.

```bash
kubectl patch deployment app --type='merge' -p '{"spec":{"template":{"spec":{"containers":[{"name":"app","readinessProbe":{"httpGet":{"path":"/"},"initialDelaySeconds":0,"timeoutSeconds":1,"periodSeconds":10,"successThreshold":1,"failureThreshold":3}} ]}}}}'
```

This looks like the safest patch to apply.

